    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Chalcolithic Age represents the transition from the Stone Age to the Metal Age, marked by the concurrent use of copper and stone implements.",
        "Distinct regional cultures emerged across India, including Ahar-Banas (Rajasthan), Malwa (Madhya Pradesh), and Jorwe (Maharashtra).",
        "Settlements were predominantly rural, consisting of mud-brick or wattle-and-daub houses without urban town planning.",
        "Wheel-made Black and Red Ware (BRW), often painted with white geometric patterns, served as the principal diagnostic pottery.",
        "Burial practices, particularly in the Jorwe culture, show adults buried in a north-to-south orientation with grave goods reflecting social stratification."
      ],
      "id": "ede1rhc"
    },
